[% header('Experimental Korean stemming algorithm') %]

<h2>Links to resources</h2>
<ul>
[% algorithm_lis('korean', 'Korean', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korean_language') %]
</ul>

<p>This deliberately limited Korean stemmer was contributed by Bonghwan Kim.
It is an experimental baseline for review, not a general Korean morphological
analyser. The same Snowball source generates each target-language implementation;
there is no Python-specific dictionary or external language model.</p>

<h2>Input and output</h2>
<p>Pass one NFC-normalized word at a time. Sentence tokenization, Unicode
normalization and contextual part-of-speech analysis belong to the caller.
The language identifiers are <code>korean</code>, <code>ko</code> and
<code>kor</code>. The algorithm returns a single stem using the ordinary
Snowball interface.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Input</th><th>Output</th><th>Reason</th></tr>
<tr><td>학생이</td><td>학생</td><td>Listed noun with subject particle</td></tr>
<tr><td>사과를</td><td>사과</td><td>Listed noun with object particle</td></tr>
<tr><td>먹었다</td><td>먹다</td><td>Listed inflection</td></tr>
<tr><td>공부했다</td><td>공부하다</td><td>Listed contracted inflection</td></tr>
<tr><td>고양이</td><td>고양이</td><td>Preserve the lexical final syllable</td></tr>
<tr><td>걸었다</td><td>걸었다</td><td>Context is required to choose a lemma</td></tr>
<tr><td>철수가</td><td>철수가</td><td>Unlisted base is preserved</td></tr>
<tr><td>먹는다</td><td>먹는다</td><td>Unlisted inflection is preserved</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Why the rules are conservative</h2>
<p>Blindly removing final 이, 가, 을 or 를 damages words such as 고양이,
국가, 사과 and 마을. The noun routine therefore accepts only complete
base-plus-particle paths for 24 noun or pronoun bases, including the selected
particle allomorphs. It does not infer a noun class for an unseen word.</p>
<p>The verb routine maps explicitly enumerated inflections of 13 verb or
adjective lemmas to dictionary forms. These selected regular, irregular and
contracted forms let retrieval match cases such as 공부했다 and 공부하다
without introducing a general conjugation engine. This is a bounded design
choice; it is not evidence that handling every irregular verb is desirable
or that the present coverage is sufficient for general Korean search.</p>
<p>걸었다 and 들었다 can represent different verbs. 나는 and the pronoun
contractions 내가, 제가, 네가 and 누가 are also preserved by the initial
policy. Context-free listed forms can still collide with nouns: <code>보고</code>
becomes <code>보다</code> even when it means a report, and <code>작고</code>
becomes <code>작다</code> even when it denotes a death. These are known lexical
over-stemming risks; the listed mappings do not perform part-of-speech analysis.
Additional particles, particle chains, unlisted inflections, mixed
script words and unknown words are retained. Stop-word removal is separate:
the algorithm does not intentionally turn words into empty strings.</p>

<h2>Matching and wide-character generation</h2>
<p>Each accepted path must consume the complete word. Alternative paths
restore the cursor on failure, so a recognized segment inside an unknown
word is not silently replaced. The verb rules use separate <code>among</code>
tables for each lemma. A single sparse Hangul table can exceed the C/C++
generator's signed-short offset range in wide-character mode; the split
preserves the same matching semantics within that range.</p>

<h2>Vocabulary and testing</h2>
<p>The <a href="https://github.com/snowballstem/snowball-data/tree/main/korean">test
vocabulary</a> contains 49,336 unique inputs: 49,239 words extracted from a documented
Korean Wikipedia shard, supplemented by 216 authored regression cases (97
additional unique words). Source, extraction settings
and licenses are recorded in <code>korean/COPYING</code>. This shard is not the
whole Korean Wikipedia and is not a balanced general Korean corpus.</p>
<p>The expected outputs generated for natural vocabulary provide a regression
baseline, not independent linguistic gold labels. The small explicit rule set
changed 87 of the 49,239 natural word types (about 0.18%) in the documented
snapshot. This is a transformation rate, not recall or accuracy: many unchanged
words need no stemming, while unlisted inflections remain unsupported. No
general-language accuracy or recall claim is made. Separate positive, boundary, ambiguous, and unknown-word cases
check the intended conservative behavior.</p>
<p>With sibling <code>snowball</code> and <code>snowball-data</code> checkouts,
use the standard <code>make check</code>, <code>make check_python</code>,
<code>make check_js</code> and <code>make check_cxx</code> paths. Build C++ wide
separately with <code>SNOWBALL_WIDE=1</code>; the existing wide CLI runner
requires a Linux UTF-8 locale. There is no Korean-only build or runtime dependency.</p>

